# 1. UNDERSTAND THE CORE OBJECTIVE

We are building a visual editing system in which administrators can customize the actual storefront using direct manipulation.

Administrators should eventually be able to:

1. Select any supported visual element.
2. Drag elements to rearrange or reposition them.
3. Resize elements using corner and edge handles.
4. Change shape, corner radius, clipping, and aspect ratios.
5. Adjust product and category card dimensions.
6. Switch between grid, bento, masonry, carousel, and other supported layouts.
7. Drag grid boundaries to modify row and column sizes.
8. Adjust typography, colors, backgrounds, images, and spacing.
9. Customize existing hero animations and celestial visuals.
10. View immediate changes in the main canvas and a miniature preview.
11. Edit mobile, tablet, and desktop designs.
12. Save drafts and safely publish completed designs.

**MOST IMPORTANT REQUIREMENT:**

The visual editor must render the same existing landing-page components used by the public storefront.

We do not want an independently reconstructed page that merely resembles the original website.

The editor and public storefront must ultimately share one rendering architecture.

Therefore, before changing the existing rendering system, we need a dependable record of how the original website looks and behaves.

The central objective of this prompt is to establish that reference.

